{"translation": "Hence none of the parts undergoing scirrhous hardening is cured either by the drugs that dry vehemently, or by those that heat strongly, [727] or by those capable of both. For the drugs that heat vehemently, having violently dispersed the moisture lying alongside and contained in the parts, dry out what remains, so that it becomes incurable. The drugs that dry vehemently, even if they do not heat, and not through the intermediate step of evacuating the fine-particled part, nevertheless bring the flux wedged in the scirrhous parts to the extreme of dryness, directly and in accordance with their own nature. Only those drugs, then, which heat, but not vehemently, and at the same time do not dry strongly either, cure such conditions. Such drugs are called softening, since they do both things at one and the same time: they liquefy what has been congealed and disperse it little by little. All such drugs, then, are hot and not very dry. They differ from the suppurative drugs in being hotter and drier than these.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 20, "surface": "ἄμφω", "english": "both", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 41, "surface": "ἀνίατον", "english": "incurable", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 107, "surface": "πεπηγὸς", "english": "what has been congealed", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["σκιῤῥουμένων", "κᾂν μὴ θερμαίνῃ ταῦτα", "οὐδὲ διὰ μέσου τοῦ κενοῦντος τὸ λεπτομερὲς", "ἐκπυϊσκόντων"]}
```

