{"translation": "Since there is no small latitude, in respect of the more and the less, in hardened bodies, there must surely also be [738] no small latitude in the drugs that cure them. For instance, goat's fat has sometimes benefited parts hardened in this way, and so has that of the hen. But these are weaker and soften moderate hardnesses. Stronger, however, than that of hens and cocks is goose fat; and stronger than that of she-goats is that of the he-goat, and also that of the bull, though less so than that of the he-goat. Moreover, the marrow of deer softens sufficiently, and after it that of calf. For all such drugs are hot and moderately dry, and are by nature disposed to heat much more than to dry. Hence, if someone should at times call them hot and moist in their powers, this must be conceded, for they stand only a little removed from the drugs that neither dry nor moisten. Of the same genus as those mentioned, but stronger, are ammoniac incense, storax, galbanum, and Scythian bdellium. Of these the fresh ones are better for softening, for as they grow old they dry more strongly than they should. This, indeed, marrow and fat also have.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 51, "surface": "σκληρότητας,", "english": "hardnesses", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 55, "surface": "ἀλεκτορίδων", "english": "hens", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 67, "surface": "τράγειον", "english": "that of the he-goat", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 72, "surface": "ταύρειον,", "english": "that of the bull", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 82, "surface": "ἐλάφειος", "english": "of deer", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 90, "surface": "μόσχειος.", "english": "that of calf", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 140, "surface": "ἀμμωνιακὸν", "english": "ammoniac", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 147, "surface": "χαλβάνη", "english": "galbanum", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["ὀλίγον γὰρ ἀφέστηκε μήτε ξηραινόντων μήθ’ ὑγραινόντων", "ἀμμωνιακὸν θυμίαμα"]}
```

