{"translation": "And for this reason, whenever ash moistened with water is strained in due measure through certain rare bodies, the hot and [630] pungent parts are carried off along with it in this process, while what remains is no longer hot, having deposited its fiery parts in the water. People call such water lye (κονία), analogous to sea-water and brine in respect of disposition, of generation and of power. For those are composed of water and salt, whereas the water that washes round the ash, having become composite out of itself and of the parts it carried along with it, has in this way produced what is named lye. Had we not known beforehand that this lye came into being out of water and the sooty parts of the ash, we might perhaps have supposed it to be a simple and uncompounded body.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 6, "surface": "τέφρα", "english": "ash", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 10, "surface": "ἀραιῶν", "english": "rare", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 20, "surface": "δριμέα", "english": "pungent", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 41, "surface": "κονίαν,", "english": "lye", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 44, "surface": "θαλάττῃ", "english": "sea-water", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 87, "surface": "ὕδατός", "english": "water", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 91, "surface": "αἰθαλωδῶν", "english": "sooty", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 101, "surface": "ἀσύνθετον", "english": "uncompounded", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["συμμέτρως", "διαθέσεως", "ἐπηνέγκατο"]}
```

```json
{"translation": "For if the acerbic juice clearly has the property of contracting, drying and roughening the tongue, and it was shown in the preceding discussions that bodies are contracted, constricted and compacted by cold alone, it is evident that the acerbic flavour is altogether cold. And since it also dries unevenly [639] (for this was what roughens), it would also be altogether earthy. For the watery passes evenly through everything, since it belongs to it inseparably to unite again, even if it is torn apart; whereas the earthy body carried along in the juices is already subject to being torn apart, and it is also its property no longer to come together easily. Moreover, the sensation proper to each of the two affections, once you recall it, will bear witness to the same conclusions. For the passage of sour flavours through the sentient bodies is seen to take place quickly, and that of acerbic flavours slowly. And sour things are seen to act more in depth, acerbic things on the surface. All these things, then, indicate that acerbic bodies are coarse-particled and that sour ones are fine-particled.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 3, "surface": "στρυφνῷ", "english": "acerbic", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 53, "surface": "γεώδης.", "english": "earthy", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 58, "surface": "ὑδατώδης", "english": "the watery", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 62, "surface": "ἀχώριστον", "english": "inseparably", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 98, "surface": "ἑκατέρου", "english": "each of the two", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 110, "surface": "ὀξέων", "english": "sour", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 112, "surface": "διέξοδος", "english": "passage through", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 140, "surface": "παχυμερῆ", "english": "coarse-particled", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["χυμός (here rendered 'flavour' for the taste-quality, but 'juices' in κατὰ τοὺς χυμοὺς)", "πιλεῖσθαι", "τοῦτο γὰρ ἦν τὸ τραχῦνον"]}
```

