{"translation": "Sweet only are the Theraean [wine] [649] and the Scybellites and boiled-down must, and whatever else resembles these. Oily in fruit and juice at once, in the strict sense, is that of the olive, and indeed also all the others from which oil is prepared, such as that of the castor-oil plant and that of the radish, as well as sesame seeds themselves, nuts and almonds. For all such things, as they grow old, become completely oily: the watery and superfluous moisture in them breathes off, while their proper moisture is worked up by the innate heat and exactly concocted. Now those fruits that become only oil-like are edible and sweet. But those to which some bitterness is added in the course of the change become inedible. For everything bitter is inedible, and it comes about through an excess of warmth, as we shall demonstrate next.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 12, "surface": "σίραιον,", "english": "boiled-down must", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 48, "surface": "ῥαφανῖδος,", "english": "radish", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 59, "surface": "ἀμύγδαλα.", "english": "almonds", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 72, "surface": "περιττωματικῆς", "english": "superfluous", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 94, "surface": "ἐλαιώδεις", "english": "oil-like", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 101, "surface": "γλυκεῖς.", "english": "sweet", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 110, "surface": "ἄβρωτοι", "english": "inedible", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 122, "surface": "θερμασίας,", "english": "warmth", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["κίκεως", "Σκυβελλίτης", "κάρυα"]}
```

```json
{"translation": "And for this reason there is not one kind of foods [653] or of drugs for all animals. For each of them, according to its own proper substance and at the same time its disposition, is both pleased and benefited by both; but if these things are so, the less sweet of foods will also be less hot, and the sweeter will be hotter by just as much as it is also sweeter. But if it should fall into an immoderation of heat, such a thing is no longer sweet but already appears bitter, as honey does when it grows old and is boiled down, and each of the other sweet things. For just as sweetness arises in each body in a twofold way from a due proportion of heat (some being sweet by their proper and innate heat, others by acquired heat), in the same way bitter things too undergo this: some by being immoderately heated by themselves in the course of the changes over time, and some by being fired by acquired warmth. About bitter things, then, we shall speak next.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 32, "surface": "ἀμφοῖν·", "english": "both", "uncertain": "y"}, {"target_no": 60, "surface": "ἀμετρίαν", "english": "immoderation", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 82, "surface": "γλυκέων.", "english": "sweet things", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 88, "surface": "διττὴ", "english": "in a twofold way", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 98, "surface": "οἰκείῳ", "english": "proper", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 101, "surface": "συμφύτῳ", "english": "innate", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 126, "surface": "ἔνια", "english": "some", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 131, "surface": "θερμασίας", "english": "warmth", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["πρὸς ἀμφοῖν", "ἐκπυρούμενα"]}
```

