{"translation": "For it has already been stated and demonstrated many times, [668] but it will nonetheless be stated once again, what it is to act or to be acted upon in respect of whole substances. And it seems I must recall it now as well, so far as suffices for the present matters, starting from somewhere about here. The generations of all individual bodies come from the four elements, and they are unequal and differ in their mixtures. For some have more fire than of the other elements, some more water, others, as it may happen, more earth, and some more air. From such inequality, then, the particular characters of the individual bodies were brought about: those of plants from the elements themselves, and those of animals from the plants, as has been shown in the discussions on the elements. Some things change into one another directly, others through other intermediate things. For example, earth changes into wheat and barley and the other things of that kind, and each of these in turn into human flesh; but it is not possible for the earth itself to become flesh by passing over the change that lies in between.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 49, "surface": "ἄνισοί", "english": "unequal", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 54, "surface": "μίξεσιν.", "english": "mixtures", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 55, "surface": "ἔνια", "english": "some", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 81, "surface": "ἀνισότητος", "english": "inequality", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 87, "surface": "ἰδιότητες", "english": "particular characters", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 128, "surface": "πυροὺς", "english": "wheat", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 130, "surface": "κριθὰς", "english": "barley", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["ἔοικα", "ἐνθένδε ποθὲν"]}
```

