{"translation": "For not only are they not pleasant to those who taste them, but they also display no small amount [698] of bitterness. And yet the bitter was never fragrant, nor pleasant to us either as a drug or as a food. What, then, is the cause of this disagreement? Theophrastus too inquired into it, but it is perhaps better that we also say what we know about it. The generation of the bitter flavour was shown to come about from sweet things thinned by heat, and bitter things are evidently hotter and more fine-particled in their mixture than sweet things. Moreover, the sensation of smell takes place in the very ventricles of the brain, as I have demonstrated about these matters elsewhere, and the substance of the objects of smell is vaporous. For the things that flow off from bodies, mixing with the surrounding air and then being carried into the brain by inhalation through the nostrils, move the sense. Reasonably, then, all things that are odorous are also hot, for an abundance of vapours depends on heat. Yet it does not follow at once that everything odorous is also pleasant, because not everything is akin to the pneuma in the ventricles of the brain.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 4, "surface": "ἡδέα", "english": "pleasant", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 18, "surface": "εὐῶδες", "english": "fragrant", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 31, "surface": "διαφωνίας", "english": "disagreement", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 68, "surface": "λεπτομερέστερα", "english": "more fine-particled", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 74, "surface": "γλυκέων,", "english": "sweet things", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 100, "surface": "ἀτμώδης", "english": "vaporous", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 116, "surface": "εἰσπνοῆς", "english": "inhalation", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 135, "surface": "ἀτμῶν", "english": "vapours", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["διὸ (in 'διὸ καὶ Θεόφραστος μὲν ἐζήτησεν'; possibly corrupt for ὃ or ἣν, 'which'; the syntax of the s